// FactRef object-wildcard matching (SPEC.md SS7 / the FactRef doc
// comment in engine/src/graph.rs SS222-227): "object disambiguates when
// commit's produces asserted more than one triple for (subject,
// predicate); omitted means every triple commit asserted for that pair
// -- wildcard semantics." SPEC-DRIVEN: contract written and checked via
// `forge goal` before any implementation existed.
//
// Claim under test: a FactRef with no `object` matches ANY candidate
// triple for the same (subject, predicate) -- true wildcard, not "matches
// nothing" and not "matches only a default/empty value". A FactRef WITH
// an object matches only a candidate whose object is equal.
//
// Modeled as: `has_object` (whether the FactRef specified an object) and
// `object_equal` (whether, IF specified, it equals the candidate
// triple's object -- meaningless when `has_object` is false, per the
// `req` below, so no implementation can dodge the wildcard case by
// smuggling `object_equal`'s value in when it shouldn't matter).
//
// This is a real, easy-to-get-backwards boolean: the natural but WRONG
// implementation swaps the wildcard direction (requires object_equal
// unconditionally, or requires !has_object AND object_equal) -- both are
// real, plausible bugs a fresh implementation could introduce, and both
// are exactly what SPEC.md's "omitted means every triple" sentence rules
// out. If `!has_object`, `object_equal` carries no information (nothing
// was given to compare), so the `req` below deliberately leaves it
// unconstrained rather than pinning it to a specific value -- the `ens`
// alone must hold for every value `object_equal` could take when
// `has_object` is false, which is exactly the wildcard property.

fn factref_matches(has_object: bool, object_equal: bool) -> bool
  req true
  ens result == (!has_object || object_equal)
  fx  pure
{
  !has_object || object_equal
}
